WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.550
SARA ROBINSON: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.860
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.830
So if you didn't see my talk,
From Zero to ML on Google Cloud

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.410
Platform, then stay tuned
because here's the rewind.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.795
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:19.970
Maybe you've never built a
machine learning model before.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.550
Or maybe you're a
hardcore ML expert.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.350
No matter your
level of experience,

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.545
we've got something for
you on Google Cloud.

00:00:26.545 --> 00:00:29.210
In this talk, I covered our
spectrum of machine learning

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.060
offerings, from
products targeted

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.810
towards application
developers with little

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:34.680
or no machine
learning experience

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.300
to tools for folks with more
ML expertise The talk covered

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.490
three of our cloud machine
learning products--

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.940
Machine Learning APIs,
Auto ML, and ML Engine.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.750
Let's get a quick
taste of each section,

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.990
starting with Machine
Learning APIs.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.170
Our ML APIs make it easy to add
machine learning to your app

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.510
with a single REST API request.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.420
No ML expertise or
training data required.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.840
We've got five APIs to help
you with common ML tasks--

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.080
analyzing images, analyzing
video, converting audio

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.990
to text, analyzing text,
and translating text

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.150
into over 100 languages.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.430
I highlighted our Vision
and Natural Language APIs

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:14.120
with live demos, showing how
you can try these APIs directly

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.240
in the browser.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.240
Then, we had a cameo from
Nick Hasty, director of R&amp;D

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.247
at Giphy, an app for searching
and sharing millions of GIFs.

00:01:22.247 --> 00:01:24.830
Nick shared how Giphy is using
the Vision and Natural Language

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:28.310
APIs to optimize Giphy's
search and GIF tagging.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.860
Nick showed us that the
ML APIs are a great way

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.297
to get up and running
with ML quickly.

00:01:33.297 --> 00:01:34.880
But what if you need
to customize them

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.034
with your own data?

00:01:36.034 --> 00:01:38.450
That's where our newest addition
to Cloud Machine Learning

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.510
comes in, Cloud AutoML.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.290
Announced in beta
at Next, AutoML

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.130
lets you train Vision, Natural
Language, and Translation

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:47.660
models with your own data.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.830
No model code required.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.790
In the AutoML section, I showed
live demos of each variants

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.680
in the AutoML UI.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.570
Starting with Vision,
I demoed a model

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:59.004
that can detect the type
of cloud in an image.

00:01:59.004 --> 00:02:00.920
While you could use the
Vision API to tell you

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.810
an image has a
cloud, we wouldn't

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.630
expect it to identify the
scientific name of a cloud.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.970
This model can classify five
different types of clouds.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.850
AutoML Natural
Language lets you build

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.790
custom text classification
models trained on your own text

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:14.810
data.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.360
I showed a demo using a data
set from the nonprofit Donors

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.184
Choose, which
matches teachers who

00:02:19.184 --> 00:02:20.600
need resources for
their classroom

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.520
with interested donors.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.700
With AutoML NL, I built a model
to categorize need requests

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.880
from teachers into categories
so that donors can easily

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.070
be matched with projects
they're interested in.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.250
AutoML Translate lets you build
domain specific translation

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.650
models for translating
industry specific jargon

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.504
and linguistic nuances.

00:02:38.504 --> 00:02:40.670
There are many instances
where the Cloud Translation

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.510
API will work just fine.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.960
But certain cases require
a translation model trained

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.920
on your own custom data.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.460
For example, let's say I see
the following error message

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.960
on my machine.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.210
I can summarize it as the
driver is not working.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.150
But what if I need to
translate this summary?

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.490
In our case, we know
this refers to software.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.800
But taken out of context,
the translation is ambiguous.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.770
It could be referring
to a broken golf club--

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.900
the driver club isn't working.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.610
The same sentence
could also refer

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.590
to a taxi driver
who is on strike,

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:10.820
and therefore not working.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.480
A base model wouldn't know
which domain this referred to,

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.080
and this is where a custom
model comes in handy.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.950
I showed a live demo of AutoML
Translate using data provided

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.990
by our customer we localize.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:22.850
I'm incredibly
excited about AutoML

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.470
because it makes custom
models accessible to anyone,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.010
regardless of
their ML expertise.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.350
But there are cases where
you have a custom task

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.750
and need more control over
the type of model you're using

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.579
and the inputs to that model.

00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:37.620
That brings us to the
third section of the talk--

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.060
Cloud ML Engine.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.790
ML Engine is a managed platform
for training and serving

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.860
your own custom ML models.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.470
It supports models built
with TensorFlow, Keras,

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.900
Scikit-learn, and XGBoost.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.400
You can leverage ML Engine's
GPUs and TPUs to train models,

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.050
and you can deploy your models
to ML Engine for serving.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.180
To show ML Engine,
my teammate Zack Akil

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.550
came on stage to
share a live demo.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.500
Zack and I each built
models independently

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.579
to accomplish the same task.

00:04:03.579 --> 00:04:05.370
I built one in TensorFlow,
and he built one

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.990
using Scikit-learn.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.962
We used ML Engine to
serve them, and Zack

00:04:08.962 --> 00:04:10.920
showed how easy it is to
build a front end that

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.674
calls different
versions of your model,

00:04:12.674 --> 00:04:15.090
even if they were built with
entirely different frameworks

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.480
and dependencies.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.670
Well, that's it.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.329
The talk covered
a lot of material.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:21.930
And I hope it gave you an idea
of the variety of ML offerings

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.580
we have on Google Cloud.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.110
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:26.980
check out the link in
the description below.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.063
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube

00:04:29.063 --> 00:04:31.177
channel for more great
Next Rewind content.

00:04:31.177 --> 00:04:32.010
Thanks for watching.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

